{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790414232,"nonce":"85RXDOYpMe81JIL25Q0X2pjrWDU=","title":"GC: a small heap wakes every mark thread on each collection","message":"conservative_trace picks the parallel marker when the initial root set has 256 entries or more. The conservative stack scan alone gives that, so a program with a tiny live set wakes the whole mark pool (N-1 threads, up to 8) on every collection: condvar wakeups, queue handoff and allocation that cost more than the trace. Seen in caribou's copy of this collector on Linux: 3100 context switches per benchmark run against 47 once marking starts serially.\n\nFix: trace on the collecting thread first; hand the remaining worklist to the pool only after a budget of 4096 objects, when the trace has shown it is large. Marks are claimed atomically, so the pool continues where the serial loop stopped.","files":null}]}